# Virtual Pat — Brief

**Run:** quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2
**Lens:** Solution-oriented; asset-recombination; cheap-and-shipping beats comprehensive-and-late.
**One-line thesis:** MWAA does not need a nine-figure program to become measurably quieter. The bulk of the win is a governance change and about $8–15 million of surgical work at IAD and DCA, most of it inside existing budgets and inside the design-manual authority the Authority already holds. The remaining question is whether MWAA will spend three years studying the problem or ninety days piloting the fix.

---

## 1. The asset inventory read — what's already on the property that matters here

MWAA does not start from zero. Four of the six things a serious quiet-by-design standard needs are already in the building.

- **The Airports Authority Design Manual (seven volumes).** MWAA already owns the vehicle a quiet standard would ride in. Acoustics performance criteria, PA zoning rules, FIDS density minimums, and tenant broadcast constraints do not require new legal authority — they require a numbered revision to volumes that already govern signing, tenant construction, and MEP. This is the single biggest under-used asset on the property.
- **The FIDS network, DSE displays, and the mobile app.** IAD and DCA already operate airport-wide flight information display systems plus a growing digital signage estate, plus an MWAA mobile app and the airline apps that carry irregular-ops messaging. The redundant visual/digital channel a quiet standard requires is 80% built. What is missing is the *policy* that says "if it is broadcast on the PA, it must first appear here."
- **The 2025 Use & Lease and the Concourse E opening.** MWAA just signed a 15-year residual agreement and is 30–60 days from opening a brand-new 14-gate United concourse at IAD. Concourse E is a purpose-built A/B testbed sitting inside the current CCP. Any quiet-by-design standard that is not stress-tested there before Concourse C/D design intent packages harden has missed a free pilot.
- **The Saarinen Main Terminal itself.** IAD's icon is a coffered concrete shell with famously long reverberation. That is a problem *and* an asset: the terminal already tells passengers to expect a cathedral. Concessionaires, gate zones, and the CBP FIS have all had 60+ years to build workarounds. Any quiet standard has to distinguish "hallowed volume, do not touch" from "concessions cluster, absorb aggressively."
- **The employees who already know where the noise is.** Gate agents at DCA Concourse C, custodial staff in Concourse A, the CBP officers at IAD FIS, the Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority Police Department dispatchers, and the MWAA Fire and Rescue crews all live inside the acoustic environment daily. Their internal noise map is more accurate than any consultant's first-pass STIPA sweep, and it costs nothing to harvest.
- **The board's proven willingness to embed passenger-experience requirements in tenant construction.** MWAA already dictates sign face materials, gate hold-room fit-out standards, and food-court finish palettes through Volumes 2 and 3 of the Design Manual. Adding NC/RT/STI targets to the same volumes is a category the Authority already exercises — it is not a new form of regulation on tenants.

