## 2. Five unconventional solutions, most to least achievable

### Solution 1 — Rewrite the announcement, not the terminal (governance-first)

**Problem attacked:** The single biggest lever on perceived noise is the number and length of announcements, not the acoustic finish. Helsinki, Schiphol, and SFO cut perceived noise dramatically without touching the ceiling grid; SFO reports ~90 minutes/day of announcements removed from the International Terminal after a 2018 policy change.

**Mechanism:** MWAA issues a numbered Order and Instruction — one for IAD, one for DCA — that classifies every PA use into four tiers: (1) life-safety, always broadcast, terminal-wide; (2) irregular-operations, broadcast in affected zones only; (3) flight-specific, gate zone only, and never for anything the passenger's boarding pass already tells them; (4) commercial and courtesy, prohibited. The Order becomes an exhibit to the airline and tenant handbooks under the 2025 Use & Lease.

**Assets recombined:** Existing MWAA rulemaking authority. Existing airline handbook mechanism. Existing FIDS and app channels absorb the paging that Tier 3 and Tier 4 removes.

**Cost order-of-magnitude:** Low five figures — legal drafting, board resolution, airline consultation. No capital.

**Implementation path:** Draft in 30 days. Airline briefing under the Use & Lease at the next scheduled operations meeting. Board consent-calendar item. Enforcement through the existing tenant compliance mechanism.

**Who resists and why:** Some airline station managers who use PA as a substitute for staffing at the gate. Concessionaires who lose the "special today" spot. Both are surmountable if the standard grandfathers safety and IROP calls and gives a 90-day compliance window.

**Failure mode:** The Order gets written but never enforced because nobody at MWAA has "PA compliance" in their scope. Fix by naming a specific role (Director of Operations at each airport) as the accountable officer and by putting a monthly announcement-count dashboard in front of them.

---

### Solution 2 — Turn Concourse E into a live A/B test before it opens

**Problem attacked:** MWAA is about to open 435,000 square feet of new United gate space at IAD. Every acoustic and PA specification for Concourse C/D — a program that could reach $20B+ — will be argued about with reference to Concourse E's performance for the next decade. If MWAA opens E without a designed acoustic and PA measurement baseline, it will spend the C/D design phase re-litigating settled questions.

**Mechanism:** Before commissioning acceptance, run a two-week STIPA + ambient sound + announcement-count baseline in all 14 hold rooms. Then split the concourse: seven gates run under the legacy IAD announcement script; seven run under the proposed quiet-by-design Order (Solution 1). Measure passenger missed-boardings, on-time push, gate agent perceived workload, and ambient level at the same six points daily for 90 days.

**Assets recombined:** The concourse itself, the AeroTrain data (which tells you exactly when the passenger arrived at the gate), the FIDS gate-change log (already timestamped), the ACARS out-off-on-in feed the airport already ingests. Add one $30,000 rental of six Class 1 sound-level meters and one acoustic consultant retainer, and the pilot is instrumented.

**Cost order-of-magnitude:** $150K–$300K — acoustic consultant, meter rental, dashboard build, MWAA analyst time. No capital.

**Implementation path:** Concourse E is opening now. The measurement plan has to be signed off in the next 60 days or the free baseline is lost. This is a "do it by Thursday" item if the Council wants a real number to argue with in 2027.

**Who resists and why:** United station leadership will worry about missed-boarding risk on the quiet-side gates during the test. Address by (a) running the quiet script only on flights ≥60 minutes to departure, (b) auto-escalating to legacy script if any flight is inside T-15 minutes without boarding-complete, (c) sharing the missed-boarding data with United daily.

**Failure mode:** The test proves quiet is fine but nobody at MWAA moves the finding into the Concourse C/D design brief. Fix by making the C/D program manager the *reader* of the pilot report, not just its recipient.

---

### Solution 3 — Directional loudspeakers plus painted "listening zones" in the loudest concourses

**Problem attacked:** DCA Terminal 2 (Pelli National Hall and the four attached concourses) is the archetype of a hold room where every gate's announcements bleed into every other gate. This is exactly the problem parametric/directional loudspeaker arrays solved for TSA checkpoints — a well-documented deployment at multiple US airports using the Brown Innovations SB-47 SonicBeam and comparable products.

**Mechanism:** In the six loudest hold rooms at DCA (a walk-around survey nominates them; the internal noise map from asset #5 above confirms) and the two loudest at IAD (Concourse B international arrivals; Concourse D gate cluster), replace the existing ceiling-mounted omni PA loudspeakers with a paired directional array. Paint a "listening zone" floor decal in front of the gate podium so passengers self-select into the beam when they want the announcement.

**Assets recombined:** The existing PA distribution (amplifiers, cabling, DSP). The existing gate podium. Wayfinding paint budget the airport spends every year anyway. What is new is 30–50 directional loudspeakers per hold room cluster and a DSP re-tune.

**Cost order-of-magnitude:** Roughly $400K–$800K for eight hold rooms including engineering, procurement, install, and commissioning. Fits comfortably inside the DCA CCP terminal-repairs line without a new capital authorization.

**Implementation path:** Retrofit during off-peak overnight windows in existing terminals. No airline concurrence trigger under any conventional MII threshold because it is a like-for-like replacement of PA hardware.

**Who resists and why:** MWAA Fire Marshal will (correctly) ask how directional coverage satisfies NFPA 72 voice evacuation intelligibility. Answer: keep the existing omni distribution live and interlocked for fire alarm; the directional array carries operational/boarding tier only. Two systems, one enclosure, common commissioning.

**Failure mode:** The narrow beam width means a passenger sitting six feet off axis hears nothing. Mitigated by installing paired arrays and by pairing every directional call with an FIDS visual pulse (already in place).

---

### Solution 4 — Take back the televisions

**Problem attacked:** A meaningful share of terminal noise is not the PA. It is 60–80 cable-news televisions in bars, food courts, gate holds, and lounges, most of them slightly out of sync with each other. Federal accessibility rules already require closed captioning on airport public-area televisions receiving federal funding at airports with ≥10,000 flights annually — a rule MWAA is already subject to. The rule permits mute; nothing requires audio.

**Mechanism:** Amend the DCA and IAD tenant handbooks (concessions, lounges) to prohibit audible television audio in public-facing areas except in explicit "sports bar" zones designated by tenant lease. Require captions on all displays. Extend the same standard to MWAA-owned displays in hold rooms and common-use corridors.

**Assets recombined:** The tenant lease amendment mechanism the Authority already uses annually. The existing displays. The federal captioning rule that already binds the airport.

**Cost order-of-magnitude:** Low five figures. Signage, tenant notice, an inspection pass.

**Implementation path:** 60-day tenant notice; the next scheduled tenant handbook update carries it. Enforcement via the same lease-compliance officer who inspects for grease-trap and hours-of-operation compliance.

**Who resists and why:** Concessions operators who believe TV audio drives dwell. The evidence on that is weaker than they claim, and hotels — a closer analog — have removed lobby TV audio without a revenue hit.

**Failure mode:** Enforcement erodes. Mitigate by including a "silent operation" line in the monthly concession inspection scorecard.

---

### Solution 5 — Buy MWAA the ability to measure itself before buying anything else

**Problem attacked:** The Council can produce the most rigorous quiet-by-design standard in North American aviation and it will not compile into action, because MWAA today has no continuous, terminal-wide acoustic measurement capability. Every claim about "quieter" is anecdote. Every capital decision is subject to consultant relitigation.

**Mechanism:** Deploy 60–100 permanent Class 1 or Class 2 sound-level meters at fixed positions across both airports — one per hold room, three per concourse spine, three per baggage claim, three per FIS at IAD — reporting one-minute Leq to the existing MWAA Operations Center dashboard. Cost per meter has fallen to the low four figures for airport-grade units. Total meter procurement: roughly $600K–$1.2M. Integration into the existing Operations Center: another $300K–$500K. Ongoing calibration and maintenance: existing facilities staff, one afternoon per meter per year.

**Assets recombined:** The Operations Center that already exists. The IT fiber backbone that already reaches every concourse. The BIM model that already knows where the ceiling grid is.

**Cost order-of-magnitude:** $1.0M–$1.7M one-time; ~$150K/year operating. This is the *entire* infrastructure the quiet-by-design standard needs to be enforceable, and it is 1% of the announced Dulles transformation cost.

**Implementation path:** RFP inside 90 days. Meters online inside 12 months. First calendar year of data feeds the Concourse C/D design intent.

**Who resists and why:** Nobody who understands what it does. Some finance staff will note it is an OpEx-ish spend hiding as CapEx; the answer is that it is federal-eligible under most terminal-improvement categories and can be embedded in the E commissioning line.

**Failure mode:** Data collected, never looked at. Fix by making the dashboard the Chief Operating Officer's morning stand-up screen alongside on-time performance.

