## 3. Precedents — operations that already run something like this

- **San Francisco International (SFO)** — implemented a "quiet airport" policy in 2018 targeting overlapping PA, tenant music, and non-essential announcements. SFO reports a >40% reduction in overall onsite noise and ~90 minutes/day of announcements removed from the International Terminal. Publicly reported passenger response is largely positive. [Source: https://viewfromthewing.com/san-francisco-airport-removed-90-minutes-of-daily-noise-travelers-say-it-changed-everything/]
- **Helsinki Airport (Finavia)** — discontinued terminal-wide flight and passenger paging; announcements now made at the departure gate and for emergencies only. Longest-running large-hub example of tier-based announcement policy. [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]
- **Amsterdam Schiphol** — has run a "silent airport" policy since ~2011; announcements largely confined to essential information; measured noise reduction on the airside (aircraft-driven, not terminal) and passenger satisfaction at a 10-year high in 2025 (3.84/5.0). Combined evidence that quiet policies coexist with high satisfaction at a top-6 European hub. [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/schiphol-in-2025-quieter-aircraft-more-satisfied-travellers-and-solid-financial-results/]
- **TSA checkpoint deployments (multiple US airports)** — Brown Innovations SB-47 SonicBeam directional loudspeakers used to deliver checkpoint instructions in a beam roughly four feet wide, without spillover to adjacent areas. Directly transferable engineering pattern for gate hold rooms. [Source: https://www.browninnovations.com/blog/2025/8/15/how-tsa-improves-airport-checkpoint-communication-with-brown-innovations-directional-speakers]
- **Pittsburgh International (Presley's Place, 2019) and Hartsfield-Jackson (Delta Multi-Sensory Room, 2016)** — dedicated sensory rooms are now table-stakes at large US hubs; the operating and capital cost is modest (single-room, single-tenant footprint) and is a proven complement to a quiet-by-design standard rather than a substitute for it. [Source: https://www.washingtonpost.com/travel/2019/07/29/pittsburghs-airport-is-latest-create-sensory-friendly-space-travelers-with-autism/]
- **Inspira Health Network (Vineland, NJ) — hospital analog** — overhead pages cut from ~150/night to ~3/night; HCAHPS "quietness at night" score up 26% year over year. This is the most useful non-airport precedent: it proves that governance-first PA reform, without capital work, drives a large measurable improvement in customer satisfaction. Airports over-index on capital solutions when the hospital sector has already shown that policy is the lever. [Source: https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20151117005677/en/]

