## 4. The "do it by Thursday" tier — three pilots for under $100K each in 90 days

1. **Concourse E baseline sweep (pilot for Solution 2).** Before United's opening date, rent six Class 1 sound-level meters and pay an acoustic consultant $60K–$80K to establish an STIPA, RT60, and one-minute-Leq baseline at 12 positions on the concourse. Deliverable: a one-page dashboard the COO reads daily for 60 days after opening. This is a $100K spend that anchors every future acoustic argument at IAD with a real number.
2. **DCA Terminal 2 announcement-count audit and reduction pilot (pilot for Solution 1).** For 30 days, log every announcement in Concourse C by category, source, and duration. Publish the count internally. Then adopt a Tier-3-only policy in Concourse C for 30 days and re-measure missed-boardings, gate agent overtime, and on-time push. Cost: one MWAA operations analyst plus one $15K acoustic consultant sanity check. Deliverable: an evidence base that either kills the "airlines can't operate without terminal-wide PA" objection or names its true cost.
3. **Silent-television standing order at three MWAA-owned hold rooms and one lounge (pilot for Solution 4).** Post signage, mute audio, enable captions. Measure passenger complaints and concessionaire revenue against matched control zones for 30 days. Cost: under $10K. Deliverable: hard evidence for the tenant-handbook amendment.

Each of the three ships inside the current fiscal year, uses no new authority, and generates evidence a Board can act on. They are the cheapest way to make sure the eventual quiet-by-design standard is not a 200-page document argued about for three years in the design-review process.

